  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25c-perchlike-fishes-3-remaining-cichlids-from-africa/benitochromis-nigrodorsalis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Benitochromis nigrodorsalis is a top rarity among the cichlids of western Africa. It was described scientifically only in 2001 and attains a length of about 12 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25c-perchlike-fishes-3-remaining-cichlids-from-africa/benitochromis-nigrodorsalis-2/",
      "html": "<p>It originates from Cameroon and the island of Fernando Poo. Prior to the scientific description the fish was known as \"Chromidotilapia finleyi Moliwe\" in the hobby.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25c-perchlike-fishes-3-remaining-cichlids-from-africa/benitochromis-nigrodorsalis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Benitochromis is closely related to the Pelvicachromis species and can be compared quite well with these peaceful and colorful cichlids regarding aquarium biology.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25c-perchlike-fishes-3-remaining-cichlids-from-africa/benitochromis-nigrodorsalis-2/",
      "html": "<p>In contrast to Pelvicachromis, which are cave brooders, Benitochromis are biparental, ovophilous mouthbrooders. This means that both parents take care of eggs and fry; eggs and fry are passed from parent to parent several times per day. The eggs are taken in the mouth immediately after spawning (= ovophilous).</p>"
    }
  ],
